WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.300
My stop-loss is set, so I can only lose the amount I typed, right?

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:13.005
A stop-loss runs your exit plan when a condition is met. It's not insurance.

00:00:13.005 --> 00:00:15.722
Being triggered doesn't mean you'll fill at that price,

00:00:15.722 --> 00:00:18.390
and it doesn't guarantee the loss stops exactly there.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.557
After the trigger, a stop-market order exits at market.

00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:38.924
It prioritises getting out fast, but the price can slip.

00:00:38.924 --> 00:00:41.750
In fast or thin markets, the gap can be bigger.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.025
A stop-limit posts a limit order after the trigger.

00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:06.344
It caps the price you'll accept, but if the price races past,

00:01:06.344 --> 00:01:10.860
it may not sell at all. Neither one is always better. They're different trade-offs.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.870
After setting them, check the direction, quantity and order status.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.464
For futures, also check which price triggers it,

00:01:30.464 --> 00:01:33.760
so your stop isn't placed where liquidation could come first.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:44.420
Not guaranteed.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.080
Know its limits, and a stop-loss becomes a real plan, not a lucky charm.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:02.590
Both say BTC. But one may not give you any bitcoin at all. Next: spot vs futures.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.120
Understand first, then act.
